  "content": {
    "id": "p63-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "$P(x)=2x^3-9x-27$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p63-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $x-3$ is a factor of $P(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(3)=2(3)^3-9(3)-27=0$, so $x-3$ is a factor.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(3)&=2(3)^3-9(3)-27\\\\P(3)&=54-27-27\\\\P(3)&=0\\end{align*}$$ By the factor theorem, $x-3$ is a factor of $P(x)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p63-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p63-q4-b-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hence by using long division, write: ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(x)=(x-3)(",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$2x^2+6x+9$",
                  "width": 26
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": ")",
                  "display": false
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=(x-3)(2x^2+6x+9)$",
          "worked": "Insert the missing $x^2$ term with coefficient zero. Subtract each product row from the row above it.\n$$\\begin{array}{rrrrr}&2x^2&+6x&+9&\\\\\\hline x-3&2x^3&+0x^2&-9x&-27\\\\&2x^3&-6x^2&&\\\\\\hline &&6x^2&-9x&-27\\\\&&6x^2&-18x&\\\\\\hline &&&9x&-27\\\\&&&9x&-27\\\\\\hline &&&&0\\end{array}$$\nTherefore $P(x)=(x-3)(2x^2+6x+9)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p63-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "By using the discriminant, show that $3$ is the only real zero of this polynomial.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $2x^2+6x+9$, $\\Delta=6^2-4(2)(9)=-36<0$, so $x=3$ is the only real zero.",
          "worked": "For $2x^2+6x+9$, $$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=6^2-4(2)(9)\\\\\\Delta&=36-72\\\\\\Delta&=-36<0\\end{align*}$$ The quadratic factor has no real zeros. Since $P(x)=(x-3)(2x^2+6x+9)$, its only real zero is $x=3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 29
      }
    ]
  },
